      "id": 1,
      "brief": "Tata Motors increased its all‑cash voluntary tender offer for Iveco to €14.40 per share, valuing the transaction at €3.91 billion (up from €3.82 billion). The offer represents a 33.47% premium to Iveco’s price on 17 July 2025 and a 6.77% premium to the price on 29 July 2025. The raise is financed partly by an €85 million credit facility with MUFG Bank. The acceptance period runs 7 Sept‑26 Oct 2026 and the offer will not be increased further.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "brief": "The €3.91 billion, €14.40‑per‑share offer for Iveco is Tata Motors’ final and best proposal. Shareholder approval could lower the acceptance threshold from 95% to 80%, with Exor’s 27.06% stake (43% voting rights) supporting the deal. About 20% of Iveco’s shares are held by minority investors (Davidson Kempner, Syquant Capital, Petrus Advisers). Analysts say a failed deal would hurt Iveco more than Tata, while a success would create a global CV group with roughly €21 billion revenue and 5,90,000 vehicles annually.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "brief": "Stellantis India, Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles Ltd (TMPV) and Tata Passenger Electric Mobility Ltd signed a definitive agreement expanding their partnership into advanced vehicle technology and product architecture. TMPV will give Stellantis access to its technology; Stellantis will develop vehicles for more than 50 global markets, to be manufactured in India at the 50:50 joint‑venture Fiat India Automobiles Private Limited (FIAPL) in Pune.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "brief": "Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles and Stellantis have formalised an expanded partnership covering advanced vehicle technology, product development and architecture. Building on a February 2026 MoU, the agreement enables Stellantis to use Tata’s vehicle architecture and engineering expertise to develop models for over 50 global markets, with production in India at the FIAPL 50:50 JV. The collaboration aims to boost India’s export potential and strengthen its role in the global automotive network.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "brief": "Tata Motors raised its offer for Iveco to €14.40 per share (a 2% increase) on 9 Oct, after activist Petrus Advisers rejected the original price. The revised price is the “best and final” offer. Three large investors (Davidson Kempner, Syquant Capital, Petrus Advisers) hold about 20.4% of Iveco’s shares and have not tendered, leaving only 28.4% of shares accepted with the 26 Oct deadline approaching. Tata cites regulatory delays as the reason for the raise and says the price will not increase further.",
      "enough_text": true
    }
  ],
